CALL newPutForth(52, 1, 'A tax cut is your money coming back to you — money that was taken from your paycheck before you could do anything with it. Economists who say that\'s the same as a welfare check have lost the plot. One is yours. The other belongs to someone else.');
CALL newPutForth(134, 1, 'You build a business from scratch, you hire people, you take risks that nobody else wanted to take — and the political class wants to lecture you about how much of your success you should be allowed to keep. That\'s backwards.');
CALL newPutForth(135, 1, 'Not letting people keep what they earned — through force of law — is taking. It\'s seizing. At some threshold of taxation, there\'s no other honest word for it. The government doesn\'t have a better claim to the income you created than you do.');
CALL newPutForth(136, 1, 'Show me a system that has lifted more people out of poverty, created more wealth, or produced more innovation than capitalism. I\'ll wait. The critics have alternatives in theory only — every real-world implementation has been catastrophic.');
CALL newPutForth(193, 1, 'I\'m not going to apologize for someone getting rich. If they created value — a product people wanted, a service that works — then accumulating wealth in exchange for that is exactly how the system is supposed to work. The wealth itself isn\'t the problem.');
CALL newPutForth(265, 1, 'A tax cut returns money to the person who earned it. That\'s not a subsidy. That\'s not a gift. It\'s recognition that the government was taking something that belonged to you, and it\'s now taking a little less.');
CALL newPutForth(267, 1, 'You have limited government resources. Means-testing focuses those resources on people who actually need them. It\'s not about creating in-groups and out-groups — it\'s about not writing checks to people who don\'t need them.');
CALL newPutForth(449, 1, 'A flat tax is the most honest system we can build: one rate, applied to everyone, no loopholes, no deductions, no lobbied carve-outs. Rich people pay more in absolute dollars because they earn more. Poor people keep more of every marginal dollar. It\'s fair by definition.');
CALL newPutForth(601, 1, 'If you receive welfare, contributing something in return isn\'t a punishment — it\'s dignity. Work requirements acknowledge that people have value beyond their need. The alternative — unconditional checks with no expectations — is a pretty dim view of human capability.');
CALL newPutForth(607, 1, 'Indefinite handouts without expectation create dependency. That\'s not a political theory — it\'s observable behavior. The welfare programs that actually work are the ones that treat recipients as capable of more. Permanent income support without work requirements does the opposite.');
CALL newPutForth(1280, 1, 'I\'m not in favor of letting people starve in the street. There\'s a floor — a minimum standard of material well-being below which a decent society shouldn\'t let anyone fall. That\'s different from saying government redistribution is the right tool to hold that floor.');
CALL newPutForth(1301, 1, 'The best case for capitalism isn\'t that it makes some people fabulously wealthy — it\'s that when it works, prosperity spreads. American capitalism has imperfectly, inconsistently, but actually raised living standards for hundreds of millions of people. That\'s the argument.');
CALL newPutForth(1672, 1, 'Wealth doesn\'t materialize from nowhere. It\'s created — by risk-takers, by innovators, by people who see a need and figure out how to fill it. The creation of wealth is a fundamentally productive act. A society that treats it as suspect will have less of it.');




